<Project>

  <!--
    ARCADE 11 SIGNING DEFAULTS - the half that changes shipped bytes with no diagnostic.

    Arcade 11 added extensions to its DEFAULT FileExtensionSignInfo that Arcade 7 did not have:

      Arcade 7 (this line's original)        Arcade 11 (what this branch now builds with)
      .dll;.exe;.mibc  -> Microsoft400       .dll;.exe;.mibc;.msi;.cab -> Microsoft400
      .js;.ps1;...     -> Microsoft400       .ps1;...                  -> Microsoft400
                                             .js      -> BreakingSignatureChange   REASSIGNED
                                             .dylib   -> MacDeveloperHarden        NEW
                                             .pkg     -> MacDeveloper              NEW
                                             .app     -> MacDeveloper              NEW
                                             .deb     -> LinuxSign                 NEW
                                             .rpm     -> LinuxSign                 NEW
                                             .tar.gz  -> None                      NEW

    There are two halves and only one of them is loud:

      declared by this repo + new Arcade 11 default  ->  hard build error, impossible to miss
      NOT declared here     + new Arcade 11 default  ->  SILENT content change on a GREEN build

    rel/17.3's original Signing.props declared neither .cab nor .dylib, so the silent half applied
    to this line in full. On rel/17.14 the same gap caused Arcade 11 to overwrite an
    "Developer ID Application: Microsoft Corporation (UBF8T346G9)" APPLE signature on two .dylib
    files with MacDeveloperHarden - same file size, different bytes, no error, green build.

    CertificateName="None" does not merely stop adding a second signature: the file passes through
    unsigned from the package it was copied out of, so the producer's original bytes are preserved
    exactly. Verified on 17.14 by hash before and after.

    Fix the loud half with Remove then re-add, NEVER by deleting the line. Deleting hands the
    extension back to Arcade's default and changes what ships, on a green build.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <FileExtensionSignInfo Remove=".cab" />
    <FileExtensionSignInfo Include=".cab" CertificateName="None" />
    <FileExtensionSignInfo Remove=".dylib" />
    <FileExtensionSignInfo Include=".dylib" CertificateName="None" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    These are third party libraries that we use in Arcade. We need to sign them even if they
    are already signed. However, they must be signed with a 3rd party certificate.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <FileSignInfo Include="AWSSDK.Core.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="AWSSDK.S3.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="CommandLine.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="Handlebars.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="SleetLib.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="Swashbuckle.AspNetCore.Swagger.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="Octokit.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="YamlDotNet.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="Mono.Options.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="Newtonsoft.Json.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="Newtonsoft.Json.Bson.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="SevenZip.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="winterop.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="wix.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="LibGit2Sharp.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="SharpYaml.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />

    <!-- Despite being called "Microsoft.*", these are not produced by Microsoft. These assemblies come from the Wix toolset project. -->
    <FileSignInfo Include="Microsoft.Deployment.Compression.Cab.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="Microsoft.Deployment.Compression.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="Microsoft.Deployment.Resources.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="Microsoft.Deployment.WindowsInstaller.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="Microsoft.Deployment.WindowsInstaller.Package.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ==========================================================================================
    WHERE THIS LINE'S ARTIFACTS ACTUALLY ARE

    Arcade's default ItemsToSign globs are $(ArtifactsPackagesDir)**\*.nupkg and
    $(VisualStudioSetupOutputPath)**\*.vsix, which resolve to

        artifacts\packages\Release\{Shipping,NonShipping}\*.nupkg
        artifacts\VSSetup\Release\**\*.vsix

    On this line the packages are not produced by Arcade. They are produced by
    scripts\build.ps1, which packs nuspec files with nuget.exe into

        artifacts\Release\packages          $TPB_PackageOutDir,  scripts\build.ps1:103
        artifacts\Release\VSIX              $vsixProjectDir,     scripts\build.ps1:954

    The two sets of paths do not intersect, so without the entries below the signing step
    resolves an empty ItemsToSign and Arcade fails with

        Sign.proj(67,5): error : List of files to sign is empty. Make sure that ItemsToSign
        is configured correctly.

    MEASURED ON BUILD 3067129, and this is the entry that corrects a wrong inference rather
    than merely recording a new one. Earlier ticks read that same error as "Package stopped
    early, Sign is only downstream of it". On 3067129 Package SUCCEEDED - 10 nupkgs and
    TestPlatform.vsix are listed by the 'Report what packaging produced' step - and Sign
    still failed with the identical message. So the empty list is this file's gap, not a
    packaging symptom. A downstream error that survives the upstream fix was never
    downstream.

    Ported from rel/16.11 (eng\Signing.props at c75ddb769d2d2ab897ac41449ddf1e0dcc3e157c),
    where the same two globs are what makes signing resolve a non-empty set.

    Adding globs here rather than moving the script's output is deliberate. $TPB_PackageOutDir
    and the artifacts\Release layout are read by a dozen-plus copy sites in scripts\build.ps1;
    changing the root re-opens every one of them. This file is Arcade's extension point and no
    artifact comparison reads it.

    The globs are recursive because Create-VsixPackage builds a project into that tree and the
    .vsix lands under it rather than directly in it. Globbing wide and then ASSERTING THE SET
    is the safe direction; globbing narrowly and hoping is not.
    ==========================================================================================
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <_ServicingPackageOutDir>$([MSBuild]::NormalizeDirectory('$(ArtifactsDir)', '$(Configuration)', 'packages'))</_ServicingPackageOutDir>
    <_ServicingVsixOutDir>$([MSBuild]::NormalizeDirectory('$(ArtifactsDir)', '$(Configuration)', 'VSIX'))</_ServicingVsixOutDir>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ItemsToSign Include="$(_ServicingPackageOutDir)**\*.nupkg" />
    <ItemsToSign Include="$(_ServicingVsixOutDir)**\*.vsix" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    A glob that matches NOTHING fails loudly, because Arcade already checks for it. A glob that
    matches SOME of the packages signs some of the packages and exits 0, and nothing downstream
    can tell that apart from success. That is the case this target exists for.

    16.11 joins the resolved set against a produced-packages.txt that its Create-NugetPackages
    writes. THIS LINE HAS NO SUCH FILE - scripts\build.ps1 on 17.3 ends Create-NugetPackages at
    Verify-Nuget-Packages and writes no manifest - so the expected set is taken from the only
    declaration of it that exists here: the twelve keys of $expectedNumOfFiles in
    scripts\verify-nupkgs.ps1. Copying 16.11's ReadLinesFromFile verbatim would hard-error on a
    file this line never creates, which is why this half is adapted rather than ported.

    IT NOW FAILS ON A MISSING PACKAGE. The earlier revision of this file only REPORTED, for a
    measured reason that has since expired: 3067129 and a local Release run both produced 10 of
    these 12 (Microsoft.TestPlatform.CLI and Microsoft.TestPlatform.Build were absent because
    Pack was a silent no-op), and erroring then would have stopped Sign before it had ever run
    once on this line. That packaging defect is fixed - scripts\build.ps1 stages an empty
    Directory.Build.props/.targets beside the packed projects so MSBuild's upward walk stops
    before the Arcade SDK sets IsPackable=false - and all twelve are produced again. The note
    that used to sit here said to tighten this the moment that happened, because a warning left
    in place is how a partial signing set becomes permanent. This is that tightening.

    THE MATCH IS ';<Id>\.[0-9]' AGAINST A DELIMITED LIST OF FILE NAMES, NOT A Contains OF
    ';<Id>.', AND THE DIFFERENCE IS NOT COSMETIC. Every one of the eight Microsoft.TestPlatform.*
    ids begins with the id Microsoft.TestPlatform, so a plain Contains check reports
    Microsoft.TestPlatform as present whenever Microsoft.TestPlatform.ObjectModel is present -
    the single most likely package to go missing is the one such a check can never see. The
    trailing \.[0-9] anchors on the start of the version, which is what actually separates
    'Microsoft.TestPlatform.17.3.3' from 'Microsoft.TestPlatform.ObjectModel.17.3.3'.

    SELF-TESTED AGAINST THREE CONTROLS BEFORE BEING COMMITTED, because a check that has never
    failed is not known to work:
      twelve present                              -> MISSING = '' , count 12
      CLI and Build absent                        -> MISSING = 'Microsoft.TestPlatform.Build,
                                                     Microsoft.TestPlatform.CLI' , count 10
      Microsoft.TestPlatform absent, the other
      eleven including ObjectModel present        -> MISSING = 'Microsoft.TestPlatform' ,
                                                     count 11   <- the case a Contains fails

    The VSIX check below is deliberately left a Warning. The packages half is being tightened
    because twelve produced packages have now been measured; the vsix glob resolving on an
    agent has NOT been measured even once - 3067129 proved Package produces TestPlatform.vsix,
    which is a different statement from this glob finding it. Do not promote a check to an
    Error on something that has never been observed to pass.
  -->
  <!-- Standalone validation checks downloaded PackageBasePath, not the producer's ItemsToSign. -->
  <Target Name="AssertServicingSigningSetIsResolved" BeforeTargets="Execute"
          Condition="'$(OutDirName)' != 'toolset\SigningValidation'">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_ExpectedSignPackageId Include="Microsoft.CodeCoverage" />
      <_ExpectedSignPackageId Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />
      <_ExpectedSignPackageId Include="Microsoft.TestPlatform" />
      <_ExpectedSignPackageId Include="Microsoft.TestPlatform.AdapterUtilities" />
      <_ExpectedSignPackageId Include="Microsoft.TestPlatform.Build" />
      <_ExpectedSignPackageId Include="Microsoft.TestPlatform.CLI" />
      <_ExpectedSignPackageId Include="Microsoft.TestPlatform.Extensions.TrxLogger" />
      <_ExpectedSignPackageId Include="Microsoft.TestPlatform.Internal.Uwp" />
      <_ExpectedSignPackageId Include="Microsoft.TestPlatform.ObjectModel" />
      <_ExpectedSignPackageId Include="Microsoft.TestPlatform.Portable" />
      <_ExpectedSignPackageId Include="Microsoft.TestPlatform.TestHost" />
      <_ExpectedSignPackageId Include="Microsoft.TestPlatform.TranslationLayer" />

      <_SignNupkg Include="@(ItemsToSign)" Condition="'%(Extension)' == '.nupkg'" />
      <_SignNonPackage Include="@(ItemsToSign)" Exclude="@(_SignNupkg)" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <PropertyGroup>
      <_SignNupkgNames>;@(_SignNupkg->'%(Filename)', ';');</_SignNupkgNames>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
      <_MissingSignPackageId Include="@(_ExpectedSignPackageId)"
                             Condition="!$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::IsMatch('$(_SignNupkgNames)', ';%(Identity)\.[0-9]'))" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <Error Condition="'@(_SignNupkg)' == ''"
           Text="No packages resolved into ItemsToSign. Searched $(_ServicingPackageOutDir)**\*.nupkg. Create-NugetPackages writes there; signing must not run before packaging." />

    <Error Condition="'@(_MissingSignPackageId)' != ''"
           Text="Expected packages did NOT reach the signing set: @(_MissingSignPackageId->'%(Identity)', ', '). Resolved @(_SignNupkg->Count()) of @(_ExpectedSignPackageId->Count()) from $(_ServicingPackageOutDir)**\*.nupkg: @(_SignNupkg->'%(Filename)%(Extension)', ', '). Signing a partial set produces a drop where some packages are signed and some are not, and nothing downstream can tell that apart from success." />

    <Message Importance="high" Text="Signing set resolved, $(_ServicingPackageOutDir): @(_SignNupkg->Count()) packages: @(_SignNupkg->'%(Filename)%(Extension)', ', ')" />
    <Message Importance="high" Condition="'@(_SignNonPackage)' != ''"
             Text="Also to sign, and not packages: @(_SignNonPackage->'%(Filename)%(Extension)', ', ')" />
    <Warning Condition="'@(_SignNonPackage)' == ''"
             Text="Nothing to sign besides the packages. On a build agent that means TestPlatform.vsix was not produced into $(_ServicingVsixOutDir) and is a finding, not an expected absence." />
  </Target>

</Project>
